[2026-03-15T20:08:56.973Z] {"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_019unDRv8KB1HANKn2WSDket","type":"tool_result","content":"   200→   200→   200→## Running Tests\n   201→   201→   201→\n   202→   202→   202→Run tests via `npm run test <testFile>` from the app directory (see `skills/scripts/test.md`\n   203→   203→   203→for the full script specification). Do NOT manually run playwright or start dev servers.\n   204→   204→   204→\n   205→   205→   205→ULTRA IMPORTANT: NEVER EVER EVER RUN `npx playwright test`. You must use the provided test script.\n   206→   206→   206→\n   207→   207→   207→- The script prints a one-line summary to stdout (e.g., `8 passed, 2 failed — see logs/test-run-3.log`).\n   208→   208→   208→- Full test output and recording metadata are in the log file referenced by the summary.\n   209→   209→   209→- Read the log file only when you need to diagnose failures.\n   210→   210→   210→- Tests MUST run in parallel with multiple workers (use `fullyParallel: true` in playwright config).\n   211→   211→   211→\n   212→   212→   212→**ERR_CONNECTION_REFUSED on first run**: If the first test run fails with\n   213→   213→   213→`ERR_CONNECTION_REFUSED`, the dev server likely wasn't fully ready. Wait 5 seconds and retry\n   214→   214→   214→once before investigating further. Do not immediately launch into manual `netlify dev` startup\n   215→   215→   215→or port debugging — the test script manages the server automatically and a single retry usually\n   216→   216→   216→resolves transient startup delays.\n   217→   217→   217→\n   218→   218→   218→**Retry limit**: Limit test re-runs to 3 per failing test before investigating the root cause.\n   219→   219→   219→Test failures during development are expected (test-fix-retest cycle), but re-running the same\n   220→   220→   220→test more than 3 times without changing approach indicates the fix strategy is wrong. Stop,\n   221→   221→   221→analyze the failure more carefully (use Replay if available), and try a different approach.\n   222→   222→   222→\n   223→   223→   223→**Batch failure triage**: When multiple tests fail, categorize failures before fixing them\n   224→   224→   224→one-by-one. Common categories (data contamination, missing serial blocks, non-unique test data)\n   225→   225→   225→often share a single root cause that can be fixed once to resolve many failures. Running all\n   226→   226→   226→tests first and grouping failures by category is more efficient than fixing tests sequentially.\n   227→   227→   227→\n   228→   228→   228→## Replay Decision Heuristic\n   229→   229→   229→\n   230→   230→   230→**Before opening a Replay recording**, read the full error output and error context snapshot\n   231→   231→   231→(`test-results/*/error-context.md`). If the expected vs received values, selector name, or\n   232→   232→   232→API response clearly identify the root cause, fix directly — do not use Replay speculatively.\n   233→   233→   233→In observed sessions, 44% of Replay usages were speculative (error output would have sufficed).\n   234→   234→   234→\n   235→   235→   235→Use Replay only when error output does not contain the failing assertion's expected/received\n   236→   236→   236→values or when the failure involves visual/layout issues not captured in error snapshots.\n   237→   237→   237→Specifically:\n   238→   238→   238→- **Skip Replay** for data-contamination failures (count mismatches, \"No X found\") — error\n   239→   239→   239→  output is always sufficient.\n   240→   240→   240→- **Skip Replay** for strict-mode violations — the error tells you exactly what matched.\n   241→   241→   241→- **Skip Replay** for invalid assertion API errors (e.g., `toEndWith` not a function).\n   242→   242→   242→- **Use Replay** for CSS/layout issues where visual confirmation is needed.\n   243→   243→   243→- **Use Replay** for complex race conditions where timing is ambiguous from error output.\n   244→   244→   244→- **Use Replay** for backend bugs where error output doesn't explain *why* wrong data exists.\n   245→   245→   245→\n   246→   246→   246→In observed sessions, 100% of Replay uses for data-contamination and strict-mode failures\n   247→   247→   247→were unnecessary — error output was always sufficient. Also skip Replay for seed-data-mismatch\n   248→   248→   248→failures — these are diagnosable by comparing test expectations against seed data or API\n   249→   249→   249→responses without visual debugging.\n   250→   250→   250→\n   251→   251→   251→## Debugging\n   252→   252→   252→\n   253→   253→   253→When tests fail, you MUST follow this process for each distinct failure. Every step is\n   254→   254→   254→mandatory — do NOT skip or reorder steps.\n   255→   255→   255→\n   256→   256→   256→0. **Identify failing tests**: Check `test-results/*/error-context.md` files for readable\n   257→   257→   257→   failure details. Do NOT parse `test-results/results.json` with grep/python3/node — the\n   258→   258→   258→   JSON log reporter strips test names and error details. If error-context files are missing,\n   259→   259→   259→   check `test-results/.last-run.json` for high-level status, then use Replay MCP tools.\n   260→   260→   260→1. Announce `ANALYZING TEST FAILURE: <test name>`.\n   261→   261→   261→2. **Read the debugging guides** in `skills/debugging/` to find the category matching your\n   262→   262→   262→   failure. The guides describe which Replay MCP tools to use first and what to look for:\n   263→   263→   263→   - `skills/debugging/timeouts.md` — Test timeouts and stuck steps\n   264→   264→   264→   - `skills/debugging/race-conditions.md` — Flaky tests, parallel interference\n   265→   265→   265→   - `skills/debugging/component-rendering.md` — Empty DOM, components not mounting\n   266→   266→   266→   - `skills/debugging/network-and-api.md` — API errors, missing/wrong data\n   267→   267→   267→   - `skills/debugging/form-and-input.md` — Form validation, input interactions\n   268→   268→   268→   - `skills/debugging/seed-data.md` — Missing test data, count mismatches\n   269→   269→   269→   - `skills/debugging/README.md` — Quick reference table: symptom → starting tool\n   270→   270→   270→3. Find the uploaded recording ID in the `npm run test` summary line (e.g.,\n   271→   271→   271→   `8 passed, 2 failed (recording: abc123) — see logs/test-run-3.log`). If the summary\n   272→   272→   272→   doesn't include a recording ID, read the log file and look for the\n   273→   273→   273→   `=== REPLAY RECORDINGS METADATA ===` block and `REPLAY UPLOADED: <recordingId>` lines.\n   274→   274→   274→   If the upload did not happen, use `npx replayio list --json` to find recordings and\n   275→   275→   275→   upload them manually: `npx replayio upload <id>`.\n   276→   276→   276→4. Announce `TEST FAILURE UPLOADED: <recordingId>` before doing anything else.\n   277→   277→   277→5. Use Replay MCP tools to analyze the failure, following the tool sequence from the relevant\n   278→   278→   278→   debugging guide. Use as many tools as needed to understand what actually happened before\n   279→   279→   279→   making any changes.\n   280→   280→   280→6. Write a bug writeup to `docs/bugs/<TestName>.md` following the investigative template below.\n   281→   281→   281→   You MUST fill in every section before making any code changes.\n   282→   282→   282→7. Only after completing the Replay analysis AND the bug writeup, fix the test and/or app based\n   283→   283→   283→   on what you found.\n   284→   284→   284→8. After fixing the failure, commit your changes and announce:\n   285→   285→   285→   CHANGESET REVISION: <git rev-parse HEAD> FAILING TEST: <test name>\n   286→   286→   286→   This line MUST appear in the log exactly as formatted — report tools search for it.\n   287→   287→   287→\n   288→   288→   288→### Bug Writeup Template\n   289→   289→   289→\n   290→   290→   290→For each test failure, create `docs/bugs/<TestName>.md` with exactly this structure:\n   291→   291→   291→\n   292→   292→   292→```\n   293→   293→   293→# Bug: <test name>\n   294→   294→   294→\n   295→   295→   295→## Step 1: Evidence\n   296→   296→   296→\n   297→   297→   297→Evidence the app is broken: <describe concrete evidence from Replay analysis — wrong API\n   298→   298→   298→responses, incorrect state, missing data, UI rendering issues, etc. Write \"None found\" if\n   299→   299→   299→no evidence.>\n   300→   300→   300→\n   301→   301→   301→Evidence the test is broken: <describe concrete evidence from Replay analysis — wrong\n   302→   302→   302→selectors, bad assertions, race conditions, incorrect test data expectations, etc. Write\n   303→   303→   303→\"None found\" if no evidence.>\n   304→   304→   304→\n   305→   305→   305→## Step 2: Determination\n   306→   306→   306→\n   307→   307→   307→Which is broken: <APP or TEST>\n   308→   308→   308→\n   309→   309→   309→## Step 3: Root Cause\n   310→   310→   310→\n   311→   311→   311→<Completely explain the cause of the problem in the app or test. Include the specific file(s),\n   312→   312→   312→function(s), and line(s) involved. Explain WHY the code is wrong, not just WHAT is wrong.>\n   313→   313→   313→```\n   314→   314→   314→\n   315→   315→   315→Do NOT skip Replay analysis and jump straight to reading error messages or guessing at fixes.\n   316→   316→   316→Do NOT skip the bug writeup and jump straight to fixing code.\n   317→   317→   317→Do NOT stop or cancel a recording upload because it is \"taking a while\" — wait for it to complete.\n   318→   318→   318→The Replay recording contains the actual runtime state — use it.\n   319→   319→   319→\n   320→   320→   320→When testing the app after deployment, use the Replay browser to record the app and debug any problems.\n   321→   321→   321→\n   322→   322→   322→## Test Isolation Mandates\n   323→   323→   323→\n   324→   324→   324→These rules are mandatory for all test files. Violations are the most common source of test\n   325→   325→   325→failures (~57% of observed failures come from shared database state).\n   326→   326→   326→\n   327→   327→   327→1. **Mandatory beforeEach database cleanup.** Every spec file that creates, modifies, or\n   328→   328→   328→   deletes records MUST include a `beforeEach` hook that resets the relevant table(s) to seed\n   329→   329→   329→   state via API calls. This single practice prevents the majority of data-contamination\n   330→   330→   330→   failures. The pattern is:\n   331→   331→   331→   ```typescript\n   332→   332→   332→   test.beforeEach(async ({ request }) => {\n   333→   333→   333→     // Delete all non-seed records, then re-seed if needed\n   334→   334→   334→     await request.delete(`${BASE}/api/resource`);\n   335→   335→   335→     await request.post(`${BASE}/api/resource/seed`);\n   336→   336→   336→   });\n   337→   337→   337→   ```\n   338→   338→   338→\n   339→   339→   339→2. **No hardcoded entity counts in serial test files.** Tests MUST use relative assertions\n   340→   340→   340→   (e.g., `toBeGreaterThan(0)`, \"count decreased by 1\") or query initial count before\n   341→   341→   341→   asserting. Never hardcode expected values like \"4 visits\" or \"3 customers\".\n   342→   342→   342→\n   343→   343→   343→3. **Destructive tests go last AND use serial.** Tests that delete all entities (empty state\n   344→   344→   344→   tests) or rename entities MUST be ordered at the end of their describe block AND wrapped\n   345→   345→   345→   in `test.describe.serial`. This must be done during initial test authoring (writeTests),\n   346→   346→   346→   not deferred to checkDirectives. Placing destructive tests earlier corrupts state for\n   347→   347→   347→   subsequent tests — this caused 33% of all failures in one observed session. Any test that\n   348→   348→   348→   modifies or deletes shared data (status changes, renames, deletions) MUST either (a)\n   349→   349→   349→   create its own data in `beforeEach` or (b) restore original state in `afterEach`. This\n   350→   350→   350→   prevents 73% of data-contamination failures caused by destructive-ordering.\n   351→   351→   351→\n   352→   352→   352→4. **Unique entity names per test.** All test data created by tests (customer names,\n   353→   353→   353→   addresses, property names, etc.) MUST include `Date.now()` or `crypto.randomUUID()`\n   354→   354→   354→   suffixes to prevent cross-test contamination. For example, use\n   355→   355→   355→   `Test Customer ${Date.now()}` instead of `Test Customer`. This single practice prevents\n   356→   356→   356→   the majority of data-contamination failures from duplicate name collisions.\n   357→   357→   357→\n   358→   358→   358→5. **TRUNCATE before seeding (Neon branches).** Seed scripts MUST call `truncateAllTables()`\n   359→   359→   359→   before inserting data. Neon branch creation inherits parent data, so insert-only seeding\n   360→   360→   360→   produces duplicates. Always use `truncateAndSeed()`, never `seedDatabase()` alone.\n   361→   361→   361→\n   362→   362→   362→6. **Relative or data-independent assertions.** Tests that verify data after mutations must\n   363→   363→   363→   query current state before the action and assert relative changes, not absolute values.\n   364→   364→   364→\n   365→   365→   365→7. **Status transition tests must reset state.** Tests that change entity status (e.g., RFI\n   366→   366→   366→   status, submittal status) MUST include `beforeEach` API calls to reset the entity back to\n   367→   367→   367→   its initial state. Without this, status transition tests leave entities in terminal states\n   368→   368→   368→   that break subsequent tests. In one session, this pattern caused 14 affected tests across\n   369→   369→   369→   RFI and submittal status spec files.\n   370→   370→   370→\n   371→   371→   371→8. **Distinct test data records per test.** Each test must operate on its own database\n   372→   372→   372→   record (vendor, PO, delivery, etc.) rather than sharing records across tests. Every spec\n   373→   373→   373→   file should create its own test data via API calls in `beforeEach`/`beforeAll` rather\n   374→   374→   374→   than relying on seed data. This is the single highest-impact isolation improvement —\n   375→   375→   375→   57.8% of observed failures came from data contamination when tests shared seed data.\n   376→   376→   376→   In one session, this single change would have prevented 10+ data-contamination failures\n   377→   377→   377→   across receive-delivery, vendor-detail, and PO-related spec files.\n   378→   378→   378→\n   379→   379→   379→   **State reset for destructive operations**: Every `describe` block containing tests that\n   380→   380→   380→   perform destructive operations (delete, modify, toggle) MUST include a `beforeEach` or\n   381→   381→   381→   `beforeAll` hook that resets state to a known baseline. Without this, earlier tests that\n   382→   382→   382→   delete or modify entities corrupt state for subsequent tests. This single practice would\n   383→   383→   383→   have prevented 4 data-contamination clusters (22 affected tests) in one observed session.\n   384→   384→   384→\n   385→   385→   385→9. **No hardcoded seed data UUIDs.** Always discover entity IDs via API by name rather\n   386→   386→   386→   than assuming seed UUIDs exist. Seed record UUIDs may be deleted by earlier tests via\n   387→   387→   387→   cascade, causing failures in later tests that reference them.\n   388→   388→   388→\n   389→   389→   389→10. **Pre-test state verification in serial suites.** Each test in a `test.describe.serial`\n   390→   390→   390→   block should verify its preconditions rather than assuming state from prior tests. For\n   391→   391→   391→   example, check that the expected number of rows exists before performing add/delete\n   392→   392→   392→   operations, rather than relying on a previous test's side effects.\n   393→   393→   393→\n   394→   394→   394→11. **Password-change tests must restore original credentials.** Any spec file that\n   395→   395→   395→    changes a user's password MUST restore the original password in `afterAll` or use a\n   396→   396→   396→    dedicated test user account. Password mutations cascade into 18+ failures across other\n   397→   397→   397→    spec files (e.g., personal-info, messages) that rely on the original credentials for\n   398→   398→   398→    API authentication and `beforeEach` cleanup. This is the highest-blast-radius\n   399→   399→   399→    data-contamination pattern observed — a single password change can break 3+ spec files.\n   400→   400→   400→\n   401→   401→   401→12. **Navigate before `page.evaluate(fetch(...))`**. Tests that use `page.evaluate(fetch(...))`\n   402→   402→   402→   for API-driven setup in `beforeEach` or `beforeAll` MUST call `page.goto()` first to\n   403→   403→   403→   navigate to the app. `fetch()` with relative URLs fails at `about:blank` because there\n   404→   404→   404→   is no base URL to resolve against. Always ensure the page has navigated before making\n   405→   405→   405→   fetch calls via `page.evaluate`.\n   406→   406→   406→\n   407→   407→   407→12. **Weekend-safe seed data.** Seed data must include entries for the current day\n   408→   408→   408→   regardless of day-of-week. Use relative date calculations (e.g., `new Date()`) rather\n   409→   409→   409→   than hardcoded weekday dates. Tests that rely on \"today's appointments\" or similar\n   410→   410→   410→   day-specific queries will fail on weekends if seed data only contains weekday entries.\n   411→   411→   411→\n   412→   412→   412→13. **Use click-based interaction for custom dropdowns.** When the UI uses custom dropdown\n   413→   413→   413→    components (non-native `<select>`), tests must use click-based interaction patterns\n   414→   414→   414→    (`click trigger → click option`), not `page.selectOption()`. Assertions should use\n   415→   415→   415→    `getAttribute('data-value')` instead of `toHaveValue()`.\n   416→   416→   416→\n   417→   417→   417→14. **Validate `data-testid` prefix selectors.** When using `[data-testid^=\"prefix-\"]`\n   418→   418→   418→   selectors, verify that container/wrapper elements don't also match the prefix. A selector\n   419→   419→   419→   like `[data-testid^=\"route-stop-\"]` will match both list items and the container if\n   420→   420→   420→   named `route-stop-list`. Use `:not()` exclusions or more specific selectors to avoid\n   421→   421→   421→   overcounting.\n   422→   422→   422→\n   423→   423→   423→14. **Mandatory beforeEach cleanup in ALL spec files that modify data.** Every spec file\n   424→   424→   424→    that creates, updates, or deletes records MUST include a `beforeEach` hook that resets\n   425→   425→   425→    the relevant data to seed state via API calls. This is a writing-time requirement, not\n   426→   426→   426→    a fixTests concern. Data contamination accounted for 54% of all test failures in one\n   427→   427→   427→    observed session (62.9% test isolation score). Do not wait for contamination to occur\n   428→   428→   428→    before adding cleanup — add it proactively from the start.\n   429→   429→   429→\n   430→   430→   430→15. **Use `test.describe.serial` as the default for all spec files.** Since all tests share\n   431→   431→   431→    a database branch, parallel execution within a spec file causes data-contamination\n   432→   432→   432→    failures. Serial execution should be the default. Only use `fullyParallel` when tests\n   433→   433→   433→    are provably independent (no shared mutable state).\n   434→   434→   434→\n   435→   435→   435→16. **Shared test utility for seed data reset.** When the same API-based reset logic is\n   436→   436→   436→    needed across multiple spec files (e.g., `resetPayments()`, `resetOrderStatuses()`),\n   437→   437→   437→    extract it to a shared test utility file (e.g., `tests/helpers/reset.ts`). This\n   438→   438→   438→    reduces boilerplate and ensures consistent cleanup across all spec files. The\n   439→   439→   439→    `destructive-test-reordering` pattern was applied identically across 4 spec files in\n   440→   440→   440→    one session — a shared `resetDatabase(resource)` helper would have prevented this\n   441→   441→   441→    duplication.\n   442→   442→   442→\n   443→   443→   443→17. **Single source of truth for seed data expectations.** Seed data counts and values\n   444→   444→   444→    used in test assertions MUST be derived from a single shared constant or queried from\n   445→   445→   445→    the API at test time — never hardcoded independently in multiple spec files. When\n   446→   446→   446→    multiple workers modify the same test files, independent seed data assumptions drift\n   447→   447→   447→    and cause recurring seed-data-mismatch failures. In one session, the same\n   448→   448→   448→    seed-data-mismatch appeared in 3 separate logs because the fix from the first log\n   449→   449→   449→    was overridden by a later worker.\n   450→   450→   450→\n   451→   451→   451→18. **Audit `deleteAll*` helpers for paginated API responses.** When test utilities call\n   452→   452→   452→    `deleteAll` endpoints that return paginated responses (e.g., `{ items: [...], total: N }`),\n   453→   453→   453→    they MUST destructure the response correctly: `const { items } = await response.json()`.\n   454→   454→   454→    Using the raw response object as an array (iterating over `{ items, total }` instead of\n   455→   455→   455→    `items`) silently fails to delete anything, corrupting database state for subsequent tests.\n   456→   456→   456→    This was the direct cause of member table corruption in one observed session.\n   457→   457→   457→\n   458→   458→   458→19. **Reseed tables in FK dependency order.** When reseeding multiple tables in `beforeEach`\n   459→   459→   459→    hooks, respect foreign key constraints by seeding parent tables before child tables. For\n   460→   460→   460→    example: `instructors → members → bookings` (members reference instructors, bookings\n   461→   461→   461→    reference members). Calling reseed endpoints in the wrong order causes FK constraint\n   462→   462→   462→    violations that look like application bugs but are actually test infrastructure issues.\n   463→   463→   463→\n   464→   464→   464→## Pre-Commit Checklist for New Spec Files\n   465→   465→   465→\n   466→   466→   466→Before committing a new spec file, verify:\n   467→   467→   467→- No test creates, modifies, or deletes shared data without cleanup or isolation.\n   468→   468→   468→- Destructive tests (delete-all, empty-state) are in `test.describe.serial` at the end.\n   469→   469→   469→- Status transition tests include `beforeEach` state resets.\n   470→   470→   470→- Created records use unique identifiers (`Date.now()`, `crypto.randomUUID()`).\n   471→   471→   471→- Count assertions use flexible matching (`toBeGreaterThan`) or relative counts.\n   472→   472→   472→\n   473→   473→   473→## JourneyQA / Batch Test Isolation\n   474→   474→   474→\n   475→   475→   475→When running multiple spec files in a single batch (e.g., JourneyQA tasks), each spec file\n   476→   476→   476→must have full database isolation. Running multiple spec files against a single Neon branch\n   477→   477→   477→causes cross-spec data contamination — one batch run caused 19 test failures from shared\n   478→   478→   478→state. Isolation strategies:\n   479→   479→   479→\n   480→   480→   480→1. **Per-spec Neon branches**: Each spec file should get its own ephemeral Neon branch.\n   481→   481→   481→2. **Sequential with resets**: Run specs sequentially with a full database reset (truncate +\n   482→   482→   482→   re-seed) between each spec file.\n   483→   483→   483→3. **Never share a single branch across parallel spec files**: This is the most common source\n   484→   484→   484→   of batch contamination.\n   485→   485→   485→4. **JourneyQA URL validation**: Journey tests against deployed apps MUST use the `/api/`\n   486→   486→   486→   prefix for API calls, not `/.netlify/functions/api/`. The Netlify redirect rewrites `/api/`\n   487→   487→   487→   to `/.netlify/functions/`, so using the full `/.netlify/functions/api/` path results in\n   488→   488→   488→   double-prefixed URLs that return HTML instead of JSON.\n   489→   489→   489→5. **JourneyQA dynamic ID discovery**: JourneyQA tests MUST dynamically discover entity IDs\n   490→   490→   490→   from API responses rather than hardcoding UUIDs. Hardcoded UUIDs that exist in one database\n   491→   491→   491→   state may not exist in the deployed database, causing seed-data-mismatch failures. Always\n   492→   492→   492→   query the API for the list of entities and select by name or attribute, not by UUID.\n   493→   493→   493→6. **JourneyQA must default to deployed URL**: JourneyQA tests should always target the\n   494→   494→   494→   deployed URL, not the local dev server. Local dev server tests consistently fail with\n   495→   495→   495→   Replay's Chromium browser due to browser/server incompatibility. Avoid the local server\n   496→   496→   496→   entirely for JourneyQA tasks.\n   497→   497→   497→\n   498→   498→   498→## React StrictMode and Double-Render\n   499→   499→   499→"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"94674423-ad14-49b3-98b9-dd40b575a18e","uuid":"28ef6735-51e9-40c5-9a7d-1ae109ff1a56","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/agent/.claude/projects/-repo/94674423-ad14-49b3-98b9-dd40b575a18e/tool-results/toolu_015niFGDZi8fj9Bp5k8EaQ6U.txt","content":"   200→   200→## Running Tests\n   201→   201→\n   202→   202→Run tests via `npm run test <testFile>` from the app directory (see `skills/scripts/test.md`\n   203→   203→for the full script specification). Do NOT manually run playwright or start dev servers.\n   204→   204→\n   205→   205→ULTRA IMPORTANT: NEVER EVER EVER RUN `npx playwright test`. You must use the provided test script.\n   206→   206→\n   207→   207→- The script prints a one-line summary to stdout (e.g., `8 passed, 2 failed — see logs/test-run-3.log`).\n   208→   208→- Full test output and recording metadata are in the log file referenced by the summary.\n   209→   209→- Read the log file only when you need to diagnose failures.\n   210→   210→- Tests MUST run in parallel with multiple workers (use `fullyParallel: true` in playwright config).\n   211→   211→\n   212→   212→**ERR_CONNECTION_REFUSED on first run**: If the first test run fails with\n   213→   213→`ERR_CONNECTION_REFUSED`, the dev server likely wasn't fully ready. Wait 5 seconds and retry\n   214→   214→once before investigating further. Do not immediately launch into manual `netlify dev` startup\n   215→   215→or port debugging — the test script manages the server automatically and a single retry usually\n   216→   216→resolves transient startup delays.\n   217→   217→\n   218→   218→**Retry limit**: Limit test re-runs to 3 per failing test before investigating the root cause.\n   219→   219→Test failures during development are expected (test-fix-retest cycle), but re-running the same\n   220→   220→test more than 3 times without changing approach indicates the fix strategy is wrong. Stop,\n   221→   221→analyze the failure more carefully (use Replay if available), and try a different approach.\n   222→   222→\n   223→   223→**Batch failure triage**: When multiple tests fail, categorize failures before fixing them\n   224→   224→one-by-one. Common categories (data contamination, missing serial blocks, non-unique test data)\n   225→   225→often share a single root cause that can be fixed once to resolve many failures. Running all\n   226→   226→tests first and grouping failures by category is more efficient than fixing tests sequentially.\n   227→   227→\n   228→   228→## Replay Decision Heuristic\n   229→   229→\n   230→   230→**Before opening a Replay recording**, read the full error output and error context snapshot\n   231→   231→(`test-results/*/error-context.md`). If the expected vs received values, selector name, or\n   232→   232→API response clearly identify the root cause, fix directly — do not use Replay speculatively.\n   233→   233→In observed sessions, 44% of Replay usages were speculative (error output would have sufficed).\n   234→   234→\n   235→   235→Use Replay only when error output does not contain the failing assertion's expected/received\n   236→   236→values or when the failure involves visual/layout issues not captured in error snapshots.\n   237→   237→Specifically:\n   238→   238→- **Skip Replay** for data-contamination failures (count mismatches, \"No X found\") — error\n   239→   239→  output is always sufficient.\n   240→   240→- **Skip Replay** for strict-mode violations — the error tells you exactly what matched.\n   241→   241→- **Skip Replay** for invalid assertion API errors (e.g., `toEndWith` not a function).\n   242→   242→- **Use Replay** for CSS/layout issues where visual confirmation is needed.\n   243→   243→- **Use Replay** for complex race conditions where timing is ambiguous from error output.\n   244→   244→- **Use Replay** for backend bugs where error output doesn't explain *why* wrong data exists.\n   245→   245→\n   246→   246→In observed sessions, 100% of Replay uses for data-contamination and strict-mode failures\n   247→   247→were unnecessary — error output was always sufficient. Also skip Replay for seed-data-mismatch\n   248→   248→failures — these are diagnosable by comparing test expectations against seed data or API\n   249→   249→responses without visual debugging.\n   250→   250→\n   251→   251→## Debugging\n   252→   252→\n   253→   253→When tests fail, you MUST follow this process for each distinct failure. Every step is\n   254→   254→mandatory — do NOT skip or reorder steps.\n   255→   255→\n   256→   256→0. **Identify failing tests**: Check `test-results/*/error-context.md` files for readable\n   257→   257→   failure details. Do NOT parse `test-results/results.json` with grep/python3/node — the\n   258→   258→   JSON log reporter strips test names and error details. If error-context files are missing,\n   259→   259→   check `test-results/.last-run.json` for high-level status, then use Replay MCP tools.\n   260→   260→1. Announce `ANALYZING TEST FAILURE: <test name>`.\n   261→   261→2. **Read the debugging guides** in `skills/debugging/` to find the category matching your\n   262→   262→   failure. The guides describe which Replay MCP tools to use first and what to look for:\n   263→   263→   - `skills/debugging/timeouts.md` — Test timeouts and stuck steps\n   264→   264→   - `skills/debugging/race-conditions.md` — Flaky tests, parallel interference\n   265→   265→   - `skills/debugging/component-rendering.md` — Empty DOM, components not mounting\n   266→   266→   - `skills/debugging/network-and-api.md` — API errors, missing/wrong data\n   267→   267→   - `skills/debugging/form-and-input.md` — Form validation, input interactions\n   268→   268→   - `skills/debugging/seed-data.md` — Missing test data, count mismatches\n   269→   269→   - `skills/debugging/README.md` — Quick reference table: symptom → starting tool\n   270→   270→3. Find the uploaded recording ID in the `npm run test` summary line (e.g.,\n   271→   271→   `8 passed, 2 failed (recording: abc123) — see logs/test-run-3.log`). If the summary\n   272→   272→   doesn't include a recording ID, read the log file and look for the\n   273→   273→   `=== REPLAY RECORDINGS METADATA ===` block and `REPLAY UPLOADED: <recordingId>` lines.\n   274→   274→   If the upload did not happen, use `npx replayio list --json` to find recordings and\n   275→   275→   upload them manually: `npx replayio upload <id>`.\n   276→   276→4. Announce `TEST FAILURE UPLOADED: <recordingId>` before doing anything else.\n   277→   277→5. Use Replay MCP tools to analyze the failure, following the tool sequence from the relevant\n   278→   278→   debugging guide. Use as many tools as needed to understand what actually happened before\n   279→   279→   making any changes.\n   280→   280→6. Write a bug writeup to `docs/bugs/<TestName>.md` following the investigative template below.\n   281→   281→   You MUST fill in every section before making any code changes.\n   282→   282→7. Only after completing the Replay analysis AND the bug writeup, fix the test and/or app based\n   283→   283→   on what you found.\n   284→   284→8. After fixing the failure, commit your changes and announce:\n   285→   285→   CHANGESET REVISION: <git rev-parse HEAD> FAILING TEST: <test name>\n   286→   286→   This line MUST appear in the log exactly as formatted — report tools search for it.\n   287→   287→\n   288→   288→### Bug Writeup Template\n   289→   289→\n   290→   290→For each test failure, create `docs/bugs/<TestName>.md` with exactly this structure:\n   291→   291→\n   292→   292→```\n   293→   293→# Bug: <test name>\n   294→   294→\n   295→   295→## Step 1: Evidence\n   296→   296→\n   297→   297→Evidence the app is broken: <describe concrete evidence from Replay analysis — wrong API\n   298→   298→responses, incorrect state, missing data, UI rendering issues, etc. Write \"None found\" if\n   299→   299→no evidence.>\n   300→   300→\n   301→   301→Evidence the test is broken: <describe concrete evidence from Replay analysis — wrong\n   302→   302→selectors, bad assertions, race conditions, incorrect test data expectations, etc. Write\n   303→   303→\"None found\" if no evidence.>\n   304→   304→\n   305→   305→## Step 2: Determination\n   306→   306→\n   307→   307→Which is broken: <APP or TEST>\n   308→   308→\n   309→   309→## Step 3: Root Cause\n   310→   310→\n   311→   311→<Completely explain the cause of the problem in the app or test. Include the specific file(s),\n   312→   312→function(s), and line(s) involved. Explain WHY the code is wrong, not just WHAT is wrong.>\n   313→   313→```\n   314→   314→\n   315→   315→Do NOT skip Replay analysis and jump straight to reading error messages or guessing at fixes.\n   316→   316→Do NOT skip the bug writeup and jump straight to fixing code.\n   317→   317→Do NOT stop or cancel a recording upload because it is \"taking a while\" — wait for it to complete.\n   318→   318→The Replay recording contains the actual runtime state — use it.\n   319→   319→\n   320→   320→When testing the app after deployment, use the Replay browser to record the app and debug any problems.\n   321→   321→\n   322→   322→## Test Isolation Mandates\n   323→   323→\n   324→   324→These rules are mandatory for all test files. Violations are the most common source of test\n   325→   325→failures (~57% of observed failures come from shared database state).\n   326→   326→\n   327→   327→1. **Mandatory beforeEach database cleanup.** Every spec file that creates, modifies, or\n   328→   328→   deletes records MUST include a `beforeEach` hook that resets the relevant table(s) to seed\n   329→   329→   state via API calls. This single practice prevents the majority of data-contamination\n   330→   330→   failures. The pattern is:\n   331→   331→   ```typescript\n   332→   332→   test.beforeEach(async ({ request }) => {\n   333→   333→     // Delete all non-seed records, then re-seed if needed\n   334→   334→     await request.delete(`${BASE}/api/resource`);\n   335→   335→     await request.post(`${BASE}/api/resource/seed`);\n   336→   336→   });\n   337→   337→   ```\n   338→   338→\n   339→   339→2. **No hardcoded entity counts in serial test files.** Tests MUST use relative assertions\n   340→   340→   (e.g., `toBeGreaterThan(0)`, \"count decreased by 1\") or query initial count before\n   341→   341→   asserting. Never hardcode expected values like \"4 visits\" or \"3 customers\".\n   342→   342→\n   343→   343→3. **Destructive tests go last AND use serial.** Tests that delete all entities (empty state\n   344→   344→   tests) or rename entities MUST be ordered at the end of their describe block AND wrapped\n   345→   345→   in `test.describe.serial`. This must be done during initial test authoring (writeTests),\n   346→   346→   not deferred to checkDirectives. Placing destructive tests earlier corrupts state for\n   347→   347→   subsequent tests — this caused 33% of all failures in one observed session. Any test that\n   348→   348→   modifies or deletes shared data (status changes, renames, deletions) MUST either (a)\n   349→   349→   create its own data in `beforeEach` or (b) restore original state in `afterEach`. This\n   350→   350→   prevents 73% of data-contamination failures caused by destructive-ordering.\n   351→   351→\n   352→   352→4. **Unique entity names per test.** All test data created by tests (customer names,\n   353→   353→   addresses, property names, etc.) MUST include `Date.now()` or `crypto.randomUUID()`\n   354→   354→   suffixes to prevent cross-test contamination. For example, use\n   355→   355→   `Test Customer ${Date.now()}` instead of `Test Customer`. This single practice prevents\n   356→   356→   the majority of data-contamination failures from duplicate name collisions.\n   357→   357→\n   358→   358→5. **TRUNCATE before seeding (Neon branches).** Seed scripts MUST call `truncateAllTables()`\n   359→   359→   before inserting data. Neon branch creation inherits parent data, so insert-only seeding\n   360→   360→   produces duplicates. Always use `truncateAndSeed()`, never `seedDatabase()` alone.\n   361→   361→\n   362→   362→6. **Relative or data-independent assertions.** Tests that verify data after mutations must\n   363→   363→   query current state before the action and assert relative changes, not absolute values.\n   364→   364→\n   365→   365→7. **Status transition tests must reset state.** Tests that change entity status (e.g., RFI\n   366→   366→   status, submittal status) MUST include `beforeEach` API calls to reset the entity back to\n   367→   367→   its initial state. Without this, status transition tests leave entities in terminal states\n   368→   368→   that break subsequent tests. In one session, this pattern caused 14 affected tests across\n   369→   369→   RFI and submittal status spec files.\n   370→   370→\n   371→   371→8. **Distinct test data records per test.** Each test must operate on its own database\n   372→   372→   record (vendor, PO, delivery, etc.) rather than sharing records across tests. Every spec\n   373→   373→   file should create its own test data via API calls in `beforeEach`/`beforeAll` rather\n   374→   374→   than relying on seed data. This is the single highest-impact isolation improvement —\n   375→   375→   57.8% of observed failures came from data contamination when tests shared seed data.\n   376→   376→   In one session, this single change would have prevented 10+ data-contamination failures\n   377→   377→   across receive-delivery, vendor-detail, and PO-related spec files.\n   378→   378→\n   379→   379→   **State reset for destructive operations**: Every `describe` block containing tests that\n   380→   380→   perform destructive operations (delete, modify, toggle) MUST include a `beforeEach` or\n   381→   381→   `beforeAll` hook that resets state to a known baseline. Without this, earlier tests that\n   382→   382→   delete or modify entities corrupt state for subsequent tests. This single practice would\n   383→   383→   have prevented 4 data-contamination clusters (22 affected tests) in one observed session.\n   384→   384→\n   385→   385→9. **No hardcoded seed data UUIDs.** Always discover entity IDs via API by name rather\n   386→   386→   than assuming seed UUIDs exist. Seed record UUIDs may be deleted by earlier tests via\n   387→   387→   cascade, causing failures in later tests that reference them.\n   388→   388→\n   389→   389→10. **Pre-test state verification in serial suites.** Each test in a `test.describe.serial`\n   390→   390→   block should verify its preconditions rather than assuming state from prior tests. For\n   391→   391→   example, check that the expected number of rows exists before performing add/delete\n   392→   392→   operations, rather than relying on a previous test's side effects.\n   393→   393→\n   394→   394→11. **Password-change tests must restore original credentials.** Any spec file that\n   395→   395→    changes a user's password MUST restore the original password in `afterAll` or use a\n   396→   396→    dedicated test user account. Password mutations cascade into 18+ failures across other\n   397→   397→    spec files (e.g., personal-info, messages) that rely on the original credentials for\n   398→   398→    API authentication and `beforeEach` cleanup. This is the highest-blast-radius\n   399→   399→    data-contamination pattern observed — a single password change can break 3+ spec files.\n   400→   400→\n   401→   401→12. **Navigate before `page.evaluate(fetch(...))`**. Tests that use `page.evaluate(fetch(...))`\n   402→   402→   for API-driven setup in `beforeEach` or `beforeAll` MUST call `page.goto()` first to\n   403→   403→   navigate to the app. `fetch()` with relative URLs fails at `about:blank` because there\n   404→   404→   is no base URL to resolve against. Always ensure the page has navigated before making\n   405→   405→   fetch calls via `page.evaluate`.\n   406→   406→\n   407→   407→12. **Weekend-safe seed data.** Seed data must include entries for the current day\n   408→   408→   regardless of day-of-week. Use relative date calculations (e.g., `new Date()`) rather\n   409→   409→   than hardcoded weekday dates. Tests that rely on \"today's appointments\" or similar\n   410→   410→   day-specific queries will fail on weekends if seed data only contains weekday entries.\n   411→   411→\n   412→   412→13. **Use click-based interaction for custom dropdowns.** When the UI uses custom dropdown\n   413→   413→    components (non-native `<select>`), tests must use click-based interaction patterns\n   414→   414→    (`click trigger → click option`), not `page.selectOption()`. Assertions should use\n   415→   415→    `getAttribute('data-value')` instead of `toHaveValue()`.\n   416→   416→\n   417→   417→14. **Validate `data-testid` prefix selectors.** When using `[data-testid^=\"prefix-\"]`\n   418→   418→   selectors, verify that container/wrapper elements don't also match the prefix. A selector\n   419→   419→   like `[data-testid^=\"route-stop-\"]` will match both list items and the container if\n   420→   420→   named `route-stop-list`. Use `:not()` exclusions or more specific selectors to avoid\n   421→   421→   overcounting.\n   422→   422→\n   423→   423→14. **Mandatory beforeEach cleanup in ALL spec files that modify data.** Every spec file\n   424→   424→    that creates, updates, or deletes records MUST include a `beforeEach` hook that resets\n   425→   425→    the relevant data to seed state via API calls. This is a writing-time requirement, not\n   426→   426→    a fixTests concern. Data contamination accounted for 54% of all test failures in one\n   427→   427→    observed session (62.9% test isolation score). Do not wait for contamination to occur\n   428→   428→    before adding cleanup — add it proactively from the start.\n   429→   429→\n   430→   430→15. **Use `test.describe.serial` as the default for all spec files.** Since all tests share\n   431→   431→    a database branch, parallel execution within a spec file causes data-contamination\n   432→   432→    failures. Serial execution should be the default. Only use `fullyParallel` when tests\n   433→   433→    are provably independent (no shared mutable state).\n   434→   434→\n   435→   435→16. **Shared test utility for seed data reset.** When the same API-based reset logic is\n   436→   436→    needed across multiple spec files (e.g., `resetPayments()`, `resetOrderStatuses()`),\n   437→   437→    extract it to a shared test utility file (e.g., `tests/helpers/reset.ts`). This\n   438→   438→    reduces boilerplate and ensures consistent cleanup across all spec files. The\n   439→   439→    `destructive-test-reordering` pattern was applied identically across 4 spec files in\n   440→   440→    one session — a shared `resetDatabase(resource)` helper would have prevented this\n   441→   441→    duplication.\n   442→   442→\n   443→   443→17. **Single source of truth for seed data expectations.** Seed data counts and values\n   444→   444→    used in test assertions MUST be derived from a single shared constant or queried from\n   445→   445→    the API at test time — never hardcoded independently in multiple spec files. When\n   446→   446→    multiple workers modify the same test files, independent seed data assumptions drift\n   447→   447→    and cause recurring seed-data-mismatch failures. In one session, the same\n   448→   448→    seed-data-mismatch appeared in 3 separate logs because the fix from the first log\n   449→   449→    was overridden by a later worker.\n   450→   450→\n   451→   451→18. **Audit `deleteAll*` helpers for paginated API responses.** When test utilities call\n   452→   452→    `deleteAll` endpoints that return paginated responses (e.g., `{ items: [...], total: N }`),\n   453→   453→    they MUST destructure the response correctly: `const { items } = await response.json()`.\n   454→   454→    Using the raw response object as an array (iterating over `{ items, total }` instead of\n   455→   455→    `items`) silently fails to delete anything, corrupting database state for subsequent tests.\n   456→   456→    This was the direct cause of member table corruption in one observed session.\n   457→   457→\n   458→   458→19. **Reseed tables in FK dependency order.** When reseeding multiple tables in `beforeEach`\n   459→   459→    hooks, respect foreign key constraints by seeding parent tables before child tables. For\n   460→   460→    example: `instructors → members → bookings` (members reference instructors, bookings\n   461→   461→    reference members). Calling reseed endpoints in the wrong order causes FK constraint\n   462→   462→    violations that look like application bugs but are actually test infrastructure issues.\n   463→   463→\n   464→   464→## Pre-Commit Checklist for New Spec Files\n   465→   465→\n   466→   466→Before committing a new spec file, verify:\n   467→   467→- No test creates, modifies, or deletes shared data without cleanup or isolation.\n   468→   468→- Destructive tests (delete-all, empty-state) are in `test.describe.serial` at the end.\n   469→   469→- Status transition tests include `beforeEach` state resets.\n   470→   470→- Created records use unique identifiers (`Date.now()`, `crypto.randomUUID()`).\n   471→   471→- Count assertions use flexible matching (`toBeGreaterThan`) or relative counts.\n   472→   472→\n   473→   473→## JourneyQA / Batch Test Isolation\n   474→   474→\n   475→   475→When running multiple spec files in a single batch (e.g., JourneyQA tasks), each spec file\n   476→   476→must have full database isolation. Running multiple spec files against a single Neon branch\n   477→   477→causes cross-spec data contamination — one batch run caused 19 test failures from shared\n   478→   478→state. Isolation strategies:\n   479→   479→\n   480→   480→1. **Per-spec Neon branches**: Each spec file should get its own ephemeral Neon branch.\n   481→   481→2. **Sequential with resets**: Run specs sequentially with a full database reset (truncate +\n   482→   482→   re-seed) between each spec file.\n   483→   483→3. **Never share a single branch across parallel spec files**: This is the most common source\n   484→   484→   of batch contamination.\n   485→   485→4. **JourneyQA URL validation**: Journey tests against deployed apps MUST use the `/api/`\n   486→   486→   prefix for API calls, not `/.netlify/functions/api/`. The Netlify redirect rewrites `/api/`\n   487→   487→   to `/.netlify/functions/`, so using the full `/.netlify/functions/api/` path results in\n   488→   488→   double-prefixed URLs that return HTML instead of JSON.\n   489→   489→5. **JourneyQA dynamic ID discovery**: JourneyQA tests MUST dynamically discover entity IDs\n   490→   490→   from API responses rather than hardcoding UUIDs. Hardcoded UUIDs that exist in one database\n   491→   491→   state may not exist in the deployed database, causing seed-data-mismatch failures. Always\n   492→   492→   query the API for the list of entities and select by name or attribute, not by UUID.\n   493→   493→6. **JourneyQA must default to deployed URL**: JourneyQA tests should always target the\n   494→   494→   deployed URL, not the local dev server. Local dev server tests consistently fail with\n   495→   495→   Replay's Chromium browser due to browser/server incompatibility. Avoid the local server\n   496→   496→   entirely for JourneyQA tasks.\n   497→   497→\n   498→   498→## React StrictMode and Double-Render\n   499→   499→","numLines":300,"startLine":200,"totalLines":924}}}
